---
/**
 * Docs-side counterpart of the two <Font /> calls in src/layouts/BaseLayout.astro.
 * Both page types need the same font declarations, and they share no layout, so
 * this file and BaseLayout have to be kept in step. The families themselves are
 * declared once, in the `fonts` block in astro.config.mjs.
 *
 * Why an override rather than the `head` config option, which is where every
 * other docs head tag lives: entries in `head` are HeadConfig objects
 * ({ tag, attrs, content }) converted directly to HTML elements, bypassing
 * Astro's script and style processing. They cannot render a component, and
 * <Font /> is a component whose whole output (an inline <style> holding the
 * generated @font-face rules, plus preload links pointing at hashed
 * same-origin font files) is produced at build time from the config. Starlight
 * documents this exact case: "If you need to import local assets like scripts,
 * styles, or images, override the Head component."
 *
 * Rendering <Default /> first is not stylistic. Starlight's default Head is a
 * one-liner that maps `starlightRoute.head` into tags, and that array leads with
 * <meta charset>, which browsers only honour inside the document's first 1024
 * bytes. The inlined @font-face CSS below is a few kilobytes, so emitting it
 * ahead of the default would push the charset declaration out of range. Going
 * second also means this file only ever adds: the title, canonical, og:* tags,
 * the view-transition style and the render-blocking sidebar anchor all keep
 * coming from Starlight and from the `head` array in astro.config.mjs.
 *
 * The preload choice is deliberate and is the same on both page types: see the
 * comment on the marketing call site in BaseLayout.astro for the reasoning.
 */
import Default from '@astrojs/starlight/components/Head.astro';
import { Font } from 'astro:assets';
---

<Default />
<Font cssVariable="--font-google-sans-flex" preload={[{ subset: 'latin', style: 'normal' }]} />
<Font cssVariable="--font-ubuntu-mono" />
